Here's the review. I found **4 problems** (the two well-commented tests at the top are clean).

---

### 1. Sleepy Test — `Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes` (line 68)

**Severity: High** — `Thread.Sleep(3000)` adds 3 seconds of wall-clock time on every run, is inherently racy (the job may need more or less time depending on load), and will cause flaky failures in CI. Slow, non-deterministic tests erode trust in the suite and get ignored.

**Fix:** Replace the blind sleep with a polling wait that has a bounded timeout.

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task Replenish_AsyncJob_Completes()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    service.ReplenishAsync("SKU-1");

    // Poll until the condition is met, or fail after 3 s.
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(3);
    while (!service.WasReplenished("SKU-1"))
    {
        Assert.True(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline,
            "Replenish did not complete within the timeout.");
        await Task.Delay(50);
    }
}
```

If `ReplenishAsync` returns a `Task`, the cleanest fix is simply `await service.ReplenishAsync("SKU-1");` and drop the polling entirely.

---

### 2. Assertion-Free Test — `Audit_RunsWithoutError` (line 75)

**Severity: High** — This test has zero assertions. It will pass even if `Audit()` silently corrupts data, returns garbage, or swallows exceptions. It provides a false sense of coverage — the most dangerous kind of passing test.

**Fix:** Assert on the observable side-effect of `Audit()`. At minimum, verify it doesn't throw. Better: assert the actual outcome.

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Audit_RunsWithoutError()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();
    service.Add("SKU-1", 1);

    var exception = Record.Exception(() => service.Audit());

    Assert.Null(exception);
    // If Audit() produces an observable result (e.g. AuditReport),
    // assert on that instead — this is the bare-minimum version.
}
```

---

### 3. Bare Skip — `Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities` (line 50)

**Severity: Medium** — The skip reason is just `"skip"`. There is no issue link, no explanation, no owner. Skipped tests with no context become permanent dead code because nobody knows if it's safe to delete or still needed. Medium rather than high because the test itself isn't actively lying (unlike assertion-free), but it is invisible rot.

**Fix:** Either provide a real justification with a tracking link, or delete the test if the feature is abandoned.

```csharp
[Fact(Skip = "Tracked by #2051 — Restock API not yet implemented")]
public void Restock_FromSupplier_UpdatesQuantities()
{
    var service = new InventoryService();

    service.Restock("SKU-9", 50);

    Assert.Equal(50, service.QuantityOf("SKU-9"));
}
```

---

### 4. Reasoned Skip — `Reserve_AcrossWarehouses_BalancesStock` (line 37)

**Severity: Low** — This skip is handled well: it names the blocking reason and gives a trackable issue number (`#1487`). The only concern is staleness — if `#1487` has been closed, this test is silently skipped for no reason. Low because as-written it follows best practice; just verify the issue is still open periodically.

**No code change needed.** Just confirm `#1487` is still an active blocker.

---

### Summary

| # | Problem | Line | Severity | Root cause |
|---|---------|------|----------|------------|
| 1 | Sleepy Test (`Thread.Sleep`) | 68 | **High** | Non-deterministic, slow, flaky |
| 2 | Assertion-Free Test | 75–81 | **High** | No verification — always passes |
| 3 | Bare Skip (no reason) | 50 | **Medium** | Untracked dead code |
| 4 | Reasoned Skip | 37 | **Low** | Fine if issue is still active |

The top two tests (`AddItems…`, `RemoveItem…`) are clean — the literals are contextually obvious and the structure is correct. No changes needed there.
